<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newport, Rhode Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Newport is a city on Aquidneck Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> Bay, Rhode Island. Founded in 1639 by settlers including William Coddington and John Clarke, it became one of the leading ports of colonial America, prospering from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>, including the slave trade, and was home to an early Jewish community with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touro-synagogue">Touro Synagogue</a>. In the 19th century it became a summer resort for wealthy Americans, known for its mansions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island.jpg" alt="Newport, Rhode Island" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MVASCO · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newport_Rhode_Island_Aerial_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jeshuat Israel congregation dedicated the Touro Synagogue in <b>Newport, Rhode Island</b>, designed by Peter Harrison and built from 1759 under the leadership of Isaac Touro.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Anne Hutchinson's followers founded Portsmouth in 1638, a group split off to found <b>Newport</b> in 1639, and Samuel Gorton founded Warwick in 1642.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li></ul></section>
